**Theorem 55.2 (uniform-kernel transport of observable agreement).** Suppose two
sources have identical visible responses along a finite seed event $E$ and the
same common rule is used. Then the complete output and resource record on $E$
is identical at the two sources. Any subprobability law transported by $E$ is
therefore dominated by both source laws. The greatest such common subprobability
is the meet of the two output laws on the declared measurable carrier.

**Proof.** Induct over the finite event: the common seed and equal response
prefix give the same next action, then the same next response and update. The
output record is equal on $E$. Its restricted law is dominated by each source
law; taking the greatest common dominated measure gives the meet. ∎

This is the stochastic counterpart of same-source event preservation. It does
not say that all sourcewise optimal resource tails are jointly attainable; the
common seed and one rule are the joint-realizability condition.

